add([1],'unpursued implication',[2],'3–9','Nous prendrons comme prétexte la théorie des fonctions L','Programme: develop torsion étale duality, Künneth and Lefschetz; extend to l-adic coefficients; apply to L-functions.','No chronology or completion inferred; the reported scans98–146 are outside this part.','agrees','The three tasks are explicit; later-package completion is only reported contextual evidence.')
add([2],'unpursued implication',[2,4],'22–42;96–130','La famille des foncteurs fibres est conservative','For a scheme X, geometric points form a conservative family on X_et; F_x is the filtered colimit over pointed étale neighbourhoods and is computed on the strict localization. For qcqs f, higher-direct-image stalks use strict-local base change.','The precise strict-henselization colimit and cohomology statement clarify compressed notation; the comparison with ordinary topology is a methodological remark.','agrees','The note correctly separates enough geometric points from strict-local fibre calculus.')
add([3,45],'unproved claim',[4,6,44],'128–175;1144–1161','commute à tt changement de base','Proper f and any torsion abelian étale sheaf satisfy arbitrary base change. For qcqs f, a smooth base-change map suffices when torsion orders are invertible on the base. The derived comparison is an isomorphism when its cohomology-sheaf maps are.','Modern notation g*Rf*F→Rf′*h*F; source uses starred inverse images exact for constant coefficients. Distinct hypotheses retained.','agrees','The theorem is stated at4–6;44 constructs the arrow and gives the cohomology-detection argument, rather than another proof of proper base change.')
add([4,5,13],'withdrawn in the same text',[6,8],'169–181;205–222','propre [deleted]; de type fini','For complex algebraic schemes the text asserts torsion étale/analytic cohomology comparison and enlarges the relative statement from proper to finite type. Its categorical margin is valid only with a suitable algebraic constructibility convention; arbitrary analytic strata give a false reading. The analytic-local presentation at6 and8 is cancelled before a live proof request.','The exponential graph in A²(C) is an earlier-run counterexample to the broad analytic-strata reading, not an authorial example. No cancellation is treated as a mathematical disproof.','agrees','All reported alterations are in the transcription. The analytic graph tests an unstated convention, not the standard restricted comparison theorem.')
add([6,8],'self-flagged step',[8],'209–234','À prouver que ce sont des isomorphismes','For a finite-type relative scheme over a complex analytic base, the proposed comparison maps on cohomology and relative higher direct images should be isomorphisms; shrinking analytic neighbourhoods should compute cohomology over the analytic local ring.','The relative étale category and analytification functor are not defined on this leaf; standard modern hypotheses cannot be retroactively inserted as his statement.','agrees','Both displayed maps are covered by the proof request; the neighbourhood conclusion is explicitly conditional.')
add([7],'conjecture',[8],'224','Devrait aussi se généraliser au contexte rigide-analytique','A rigid-analytic analogue of the preceding relative étale comparison is expected.','No base field, valuation, coefficient restrictions or analytic realization is specified; only later precisely formulated comparison theorems can be matched.','agrees','This is an expectation with incomplete hypotheses, not a proved rigid comparison.')
add([9],'unproved claim',[10,48],'280;1251–1258','les R^qf_*(F) ... sont loc. libres','For proper smooth f and prime-to-residue-characteristic locally constant torsion coefficients, higher direct images are locally constant. Literal freeness over a fixed nonfield coefficient ring is stronger and is not guaranteed merely by F being free.','The note repeats locally free without examining the coefficient-ring meaning; modern local constancy is the safe reconstruction. A nonsemisimple finite coefficient local system supplies a freeness counterexample.','understates','The note preserves the important coefficient warning but does not test the actual loc. libres wording. It must be separated from local constancy.')
add([10],'reading question',[10],'282–296','i >|≥ 2n','For X of finite type over a separably closed field, torsion étale cohomology vanishes above2dimX; for affine X it vanishes above dimX.','Strictness of the first inequality is mathematically forced by nonzero top cohomology of projective space, but the ink remains undecided.','agrees','Absolute and affine bounds are distinct from relative bounds and Tor amplitude.')
add([11,14],'unproved claim',[12],'318–355','Suite ... exacte ... spectrale','For closed i:Y→X with open complement j, sheaves supported on Y are direct images of sheaves on Y; RΓ_Y is derived sections with support, with localization triangle and local-to-global spectral sequence.','The exact triangle and spectral sequence are modern reconstructions of bare headings. Set-valued sheaves trivial on the complement require the terminal rather than zero object.','agrees','The displayed Ext identities and low-degree kernel/cokernel assertions are present; full sequences are not written.')
add([12,24,26,27,28,29],'unproved claim',[12,22,24,26],'371;575–598;626–646;690–704','on prouve que cette section ne dépend pas du choix des Y_i','For a regular immersion Y⊂X of codimension d with n invertible, local divisor equations give a Kummer class, whose d-fold cup product is asserted independent of regular equations, compatible with admissible base changes, intersections without excess and coefficient changes.','Elementary-generator tests and the l-adic transition test are ideas of the earlier run. Derived inverse-limit control is additional to finite-level compatibility. The requested definition at12 is supplied at22–24; its independence is only asserted.','agrees','All steps and their different proof statuses are distinguished; the notes do not claim to have completed generator invariance.')
add([15,38],'self-flagged step',[12,14,26],'358–371;407–409;708–720','Pb Pureté absolue','Geometric purity for smooth pairs predicts R^qi!Z/n=0 except q=2d, where it is Z/n(-d). The boxed problem seeks the regular-scheme analogue with n invertible; the text gives equal-characteristic/resolution qualifications.','The modern regular closed immersion theorem is a reconstruction with explicit regular/noetherian and invertibility hypotheses. The terse base-change proof indication does not prove absolute purity.','agrees','The boxed problem is explicit and separate from a local-model proof sketch for geometric purity.')
